###################
# Britain
###################

# Great Britain introduced Greenwich Mean Time GMT as 'Railway Time' beginning in
# the 1840ies. Its adoption thoughout the country was fast, but not uniform.
# Shanks and Gabriel list different dates of GMT introduction for different areas.
# To create separate zones for these areas is mostly an academic matter, as
# little practical consequences arise. The difference between LMT and GMT 
# in England is at most 22 minutes for Land's End in Cornwall, and much less
# for the main body of England.
# Sources: The Sociology of Time, ed. John Hassard (1990)


# Zone	NAME		STDOFF	RULES	FORMAT	[UNTIL]		#azn=55
#Zone	Europe/London	-0:01:15 -	LMT	1847 Dec  1  0:00s
#			 0:00	GB-Eire	%s	1968 Oct 27
#			 1:00	-	BST	1971 Oct 31  2:00u
#			 0:00	GB-Eire	%s	1996
#			 0:00	EU	GMT/BST

# a discontinuous spread, al of Cornwall, around Chelmsford, Cambridge, Oxford
# Oxford may be the largest town
Zone	Europe/Britain/Oxford -0:05:00 -	LMT	1855 Jan 1  #azn=56
			0	follow	Europe/London

Zone	Europe/Britain/Bristol -0:10:20 -	LMT	1852 Sep 14 #azn=57
			0	follow	Europe/London

# see  https://www.devonheritage.org/Places/Exeter/ExeterLocalTime.htm
Zone	Europe/Britain/Exeter -0:14:04 -	LMT	1852 Nov 2 #azn=58
			0	follow	Europe/London

# a court case in 1858 made LMT the legal time until formal GMT law in 1880
Zone	Europe/Britain/Dorchester -0:09:44 -	LMT	1880 Aug 2 #azn=59
			0	follow	Europe/London

# Scotland introduced GMT on 30 Jan 1848
Zone	Europe/Britain/Glasgow -0:09:44 -	LMT	1848 Jan 30 #azn=70
			0	follow	Europe/London

# John Hassard lists towns in Wales which kept local time in 1852 or longer
# Brecon, Milford Haven, 
# is identical to Oxford	# azn=73

